WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:04.060
We've all lost it at one point or another
during an emotional film.

00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:09.030
Sometimes, in just about any movie, all it
takes is a well-timed moment or a single line,

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:10.620
and bam — waterworks.

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:18.400
But the scenes in these movies in particular
crank the sadness up to 11, leaving the audience

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:20.500
practically swimming by the time the credits
roll.

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:24.800
Clear your throat, because we're about to
put a lump in it with the most emotional movies

00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:25.500
of all time.

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:26.940
Interstellar

00:00:27.620 --> 00:00:31.100
If you've caught Christopher Nolan's space
epic Interstellar, you'll know what's coming

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:31.600
next.

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:35.740
This story of headstrong astronaut Joseph
Cooper, played by Matthew McConaughey, isn't

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:39.880
one you'd anticipate to have you bawling like
a baby, but oh, it absolutely does.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:43.940
With Earth facing extinction, Cooper and the
crew of the spaceship Endurance head out in

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:46.440
an attempt to find a suitable planet to colonize.

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:50.760
But after getting stranded on one planet for
decades of relative time, Cooper arrives back

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:54.730
on the Endurance to years' worth of messages
from his children, Murphy and Tom.

00:00:54.730 --> 00:00:57.540
They were just kids when he left Earth, but
they're now adults.

00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:01.680
Compound this with McConaughey's gripping
performance, and this scene leaves you decidedly

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:04.040
not "alright, alright, alright."

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:06.180
Guardians of the Galaxy

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:11.000
For all its high-throttle space action and
hilarious one-liners, Guardians of the Galaxy

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:15.240
still managed to deliver a rich, emotional
story and a couple moments that just broke

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:16.380
the tear bank completely.

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:20.440
One of the most heartfelt parts of the movie
comes right in the opening scene, which sees

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:22.600
young Peter Quill with his mother on her deathbed.

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:26.540
It's bad enough when Peter refuses to take
her hand seconds before she flatlines.

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:30.600
But let's face it — we all lost it when
Peter's grandfather has to pull him screaming

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:31.180
from the room.

00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:32.540
"No."

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:38.320
"No!"

00:01:38.660 --> 00:01:42.420
Geez, not even five minutes into the movie,
and you're already sobbing into your Milk

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:42.920
Duds.

00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:44.420
The Green Mile

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:49.800
Adapted from the 1996 Stephen King novel,
The Green Mile seems like a dark film on the

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:54.200
surface, but what lies below is a web of relationships
that will tug at your heartstrings.

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:55.080
The most touching of all?

00:01:55.260 --> 00:02:00.020
John Coffey, played by the late Michael Clarke
Duncan, and Paul Edgecomb, played by Tom Hanks,

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:04.620
who form an unusual alliance at Cold Mountain
Penitentiary, where John is awaiting execution

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:06.090
for a crime he didn't commit.

00:02:06.090 --> 00:02:09.720
While The Green Mile has plenty of emotional
moments, one in particular gets the tears

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:11.040
flowing every time.

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:15.540
Even though he's an innocent man, John confides
in Paul that he wants to die, because the

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:17.680
world is too cruel a place for him to live.

00:02:17.860 --> 00:02:22.260
Moments before John is killed, Paul steps
forward to shake his hand, a subtle reminder

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:24.980
that their bond was true, and a thank you
for sending him onward.

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:27.060
The Imitation Game

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:31.920
This historical drama brings light to one
of Britain's most brilliant minds: Alan Turing,

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:36.120
the mathematician who became famous for his
efforts to crack the code on the German Enigma

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:37.520
machines during World War II.

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:41.580
Despite his achievements, Turing was subject
to extreme ridicule by the United Kingdom's

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:45.340
legal system following the discovery that
he was in a same-sex relationship with a young

00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:45.840
man.

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:49.700
The Imitation Game depicts this arresting
account of discrimination very well, but the

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:51.240
tipping point comes near the end.

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:55.060
Facing the risk of losing his latest machine,
which he named Christopher after a boy he

00:02:55.069 --> 00:02:59.010
fell in love with in school, Turing breaks
down in one of the most emotional onscreen

00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:00.880
performances in recent years.

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:02.420
Toy Story 3

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:06.940
Pixar has a particular way of pulling at your
emotions, but there was something uniquely

00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:09.260
devastating about 2010's Toy Story 3.

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:14.040
Released 11 years after the second installment,
Toy Story 3 had no shortage of hilarious moments

00:03:14.059 --> 00:03:17.989
and tongue-in-cheek references, and it also
came with a plotline that drove a nail straight

00:03:17.989 --> 00:03:22.689
into its audience's heart: the gang of toys
don't belong to Andy anymore, a circumstance

00:03:22.689 --> 00:03:24.580
that leaves their fates hanging by a thread.

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:28.980
During the climax of the film, the whole bunch
end up in an incinerator after an escape plan

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:29.580
goes wrong.

00:03:29.740 --> 00:03:33.280
Of course, watching characters you were first
introduced to when you were just a kid in

00:03:33.290 --> 00:03:37.219
danger is difficult enough, but the moment
the crew realizes that they're going to die

00:03:37.219 --> 00:03:41.849
— and decide to link up hands so they can
face their fate together is next-level cinematic

00:03:41.849 --> 00:03:42.349
torture.

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:46.240
You had to have eyes of steel to make it through
that scene without a few tears.

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:47.780
The NeverEnding Story

00:03:48.460 --> 00:03:53.319
The 1984 film The NeverEnding Story bills
itself as an epic fantasy, but it could also

00:03:53.319 --> 00:03:58.140
be called a "psychological roller-coaster,"
because that's exactly what audiences experience

00:03:58.140 --> 00:04:02.860
when they witness Atreyu's horse Artax sink
into the Swamps of Sadness forever.

00:04:03.069 --> 00:04:06.980
When the boy-and-horse pair are trekking through
the swamps, Artax gets stuck in the mud and

00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:11.890
is too downtrodden to carry on, so he slowly
sinks to his tragic fate — and the camera

00:04:11.890 --> 00:04:15.339
doesn't cut away, guaranteeing no one will
step away dry-eyed.

00:04:15.339 --> 00:04:19.519
Not even a Luckdragon can make up for this
kind of blow to your emotions.

00:04:19.519 --> 00:04:21.060
Good Will Hunting

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:26.340
The late Robin Williams' Oscar-winning performance
as Professor Sean Maguire in the 1997 drama

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:30.180
Good Will Hunting is one of his finest — as
the Academy can clearly tell you — and a

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:32.200
lot of that comes down to one scene.

00:04:32.420 --> 00:04:36.960
Matt Damon's Will Hunting is speaking to Sean
about being abused by his foster father, and

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:41.800
the moment spirals into a stunning breakthrough
grounded in a single sentence that sits sincere

00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:44.720
amongst a film that's littered with sarcastic
one-liners.

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:47.080
"It's not your fault."

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:52.740
Thanks for watching!

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:55.220
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:04:55.420 --> 00:04:57.900
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

